repository-code: "https://github.com/tonydzi/claude-desktop-watchdog"
url: "https://github.com/tonydzi/claude-desktop-watchdog"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A single-file PowerShell watchdog for Claude Desktop on Windows, where a dead or wedged
  instance keeps holding the single-instance lock, so clicking the icon is swallowed by a
  process that will never draw a window again and only a reboot recovers. It reaps exactly
  two classes of survivor: processes whose executable path is outside the InstallLocation
  that Get-AppxPackage reports now, which is what an interrupted MSIX update leaves behind,
  and instances whose windows stop responding across two consecutive ticks on the same pid.
  A window ping is not a diagnosis, so the hang branch is fenced with a startup grace, a
  stale-state cutoff, a one-live-window veto and a crash-loop brake, and it fails open:
  a missed heal costs a manual restart, a false one costs the user's work. Every healing run
  first writes an incident snapshot - package status, process list with paths, application
  log tail, AppX deployment events - because the failure survives mainly by leaving no
  evidence behind once the machine is healthy again.
keywords:
  - claude-desktop
  - watchdog
  - windows
  - msix
  - single-instance-lock
  - reliability
  - incident-evidence
